  {
    tier: 'Wipro / HCLTech / Tech Mahindra',
    range: 'Rs 3.5-6.5 LPA',
    note: 'Broadly similar band to TCS/Infosys, with some variation by role track and campus tier.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'Global Capability Centre (GCC) fresher',
    range: '12-20% premium over services, sometimes higher',
    note: 'GCCs of MNCs (banks, retailers, tech firms operating their own India centres) increasingly out-pay pure services firms for comparable fresher roles.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'Product company / well-funded startup fresher',
    range: 'Rs 6-15 LPA',
    note: 'Far smaller number of seats than services firms hire in a single year, and harder to enter without a project portfolio.',
  },
];

const benchRows = [
  {
    signal: 'What "bench" actually means',
    reality:
      'Bench is the period after training when you are on the payroll but not yet billed to a client project. It is normal in project-based services work, not a punishment by itself.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'How long benching used to run',
    reality:
      'IT services firms traditionally let unbilled staff sit on the bench for up to 90-120 days before pushing them onto a project or performance-managing them out.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'What changed in 2025',
    reality:
      'TCS introduced a policy in June 2025 requiring associates to be billed for at least 225 business days a year, which functionally caps bench time at around 35 days annually. Other firms are tightening similarly to control wage costs.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'What this means for you as a fresher',
    reality:
      'Shorter bench tolerance cuts both ways: less dead time waiting for a project, but also less patience if you are slow to clear internal certifications or skill assessments after training ends.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology',
    body: 'Can you sit through a fixed 9-to-6 (often 5-day office mandate now) routine of ticket-based, sometimes repetitive maintenance or support work without your motivation collapsing? A service job rewards steady, patient, unglamorous consistency far more than it rewards visible brilliance.',
    note: 'If you need variety and constant intellectual stretch from week one, the routine will frustrate you faster than the paycheck compensates for.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your household comfortably run on roughly Rs 3.5-9 LPA for the next 1-3 years while you build proof of work on the side? Or does your family situation genuinely need a bigger number immediately, which should change which offer you accept, not just whether you accept one.',
    note: 'A services offer is a real, valid financial floor. Treat it as a floor to build from, not the number you expect to still be earning five years from now.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Top IT firms are still onboarding roughly 82,000 graduates in FY2026, and Nasscom projects IT hiring growth of about 7% in FY27. But this is happening alongside active workforce "rationalization" — TCS alone cut over 23,000 roles in FY26. The seats exist; so does real churn at every level above entry.',
    note: 'Demand existing and demand being stable are two different facts. A services job is currently a moving floor, not a lifetime guarantee.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'Routine, low-judgment coding and support work is exactly the layer AI tools are absorbing fastest. The freshers who stay valuable inside a service company are the ones who move toward client-facing, architecture, or AI-oversight work within their first 2-3 years, not the ones who keep doing the same ticket-closing work indefinitely.',
    note: 'The real question is not "will this job disappear." It is "will I still be doing only entry-level tasks in year four."',
  },
];

const whoFitsItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You need a stable first income and structured training right now',
    body: 'A 2-3 month structured onboarding program plus a predictable paycheck is a legitimate, sensible starting point if you have no other offer, unclear direction yet, or a family that needs you earning soon rather than later.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want broad exposure before narrowing down',
    body: 'Service companies staff people across banking, retail, healthcare, and telecom client accounts. If you genuinely do not know which domain or tech stack you want yet, this rotation-style exposure can help you find out faster than guessing from outside.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You plan to treat this as a 1-3 year runway, not a destination',
    body: 'People who use the first two years to bank savings, clear a certification or two, and quietly build a portfolio on the side tend to look back on the services stint as useful. People who drift without a plan tend to look back on it as lost time.',
  },
];

const pauseRows = [
  {
    signal: 'You are choosing it only because "IT still pays decently"',
    reality: 'Rs 3.36-9 LPA is the honest fresher range at most large services firms, not the Rs 12+ LPA figure that circulates in family conversations. Decide based on the real number, not the rumor.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You already have a stronger offer from a GCC or product company',
    reality: 'GCCs typically pay a 12-20% premium over comparable services roles for freshers, and product companies can pay considerably more. If you already cleared that harder bar, taking the lower-paying services offer "to be safe" usually is not the safer move.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You are hoping the job itself will teach you everything you need',
    reality: 'The Initial Learning Program (ILP)-style training genuinely standardizes baseline skill, which has real value. But it is designed to make you client-ready, not market-competitive five years out. What happens after training is on you.',
  },
];

const exitRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Under 12 months',
    guidance: 'Leaving this early, without a clearly better offer already in hand, reads as a red flag to most recruiters. Finish the structured training, clear your first project assignment, and build at least one visible proof asset before you look elsewhere.',
  },
  {
    stage: '1-2 years',
    guidance: 'This is the realistic window for most freshers to start actively applying elsewhere if the role has plateaued, once you have a project reference, a skill certification, and something to show beyond your job title.',
  },
  {
    stage: '2-3 years',
    guidance: 'A common, sensible exit point if you used the time to build proof of work. The general job-market guideline of switching roughly every 1-3 years fits this window well, and it typically aligns with when a service-company salary plateau starts to bite.',
  },
  {
    stage: '5+ years, no plan',
    guidance: 'This is the stagnation zone people describe when they say they feel "stuck." It is recoverable, but it takes a more deliberate proof-building push than switching earlier would have needed.',
  },
];

const skillRows = [
  {
    layer: 'Inside official hours (low effort, high leverage)',
    items: 'Clear the certifications your company already recommends (cloud, testing, or domain-specific "star" certifications) since they cost you nothing extra and carry real weight on a resume.',
  },
  {
    layer: 'Evenings and weekends (the multiplier layer)',
    items: 'One cloud fundamentals course (AWS/Azure/GCP), one small deployed project outside work tickets, and comfort using AI coding assistants to move faster and verify output, not just to auto-complete boilerplate.',
  },
  {
    layer: 'Visible proof (what actually gets you hired next)',
    items: 'A cleaned-up LinkedIn or GitHub, one project with a clear explanation of the problem you solved, and one certification you can defend in an interview, not just list.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Treating the offer letter as the finish line',
    body: 'The interview process ends when you sign. The career decision starts on day one. Freshers who mentally "relax" after placement season tend to be the ones still doing entry-level tasks three years later.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Waiting for the company to hand you growth',
    body: 'Service companies run on structured grades and internal certifications. If you wait passively for a manager to notice you, promotions and better projects go to people who visibly asked for them and built proof first.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Job-hopping before you have anything to show',
    body: 'Leaving in under a year with nothing but a job title to point to often looks worse to a future employer than staying and building one real proof asset first.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Ignoring AI literacy because "that is not my project"',
    body: 'Engineers who learn to use AI tools to move faster and then verify the output are pulling ahead inside service companies too, especially as firms push AI-linked variable pay and skill premiums for AI-capable staff.',
